seo Luis Adolfo Noboa Naranjo) is a museum in Guayaquil, Ecuador. It was established on January 25, 2006, by Alvaro Noboa. It displays the private collection of paintings of the late Ecuadorian entrepreneur, Luis Noboa Naranjo, representing a sample of the most important contemporary Ecuadorian painters, with whom he had cultivated friendship.","wpu":"Luis Adolfo Noboa Naranjo Museum","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/MLANN2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Guayaquil Municipal Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-2.187222,"lo":-79.8825,"d":415,"ds":"Guayaquil Municipal Museum (Spanish: Museo Municipal de Guayaquil) is a museum in Guayaquil, Ecuador. It contains artifacts, objects and historical items relating to the history of Guayaquil. It is considered the most important of the city and one of the best in the country.","wpu":"Guayaquil Municipal Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Museo_municipal_de_Guayaquil_2020.jpg/960px-Museo_municipal_de_Guayaquil_2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Velero Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-2.188889,"lo":-79.901111,"d":2348,"ds":"Velero Bridge, also known as \"El Velero\", (Spanish: Puente del Velero) is a bridge in Guayaquil, Ecuador. One of the prominent landmarks of the city, especially when lit up at night, it was inaugurated on July 27, 2005. It forms part of the route of the Guayaquil Marathon.","wpu":"Velero Bridge","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cd/Xlatlrah-1247152506-bg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Santay Island","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-2.2181,"lo":-79.8536,"d":4285,"ds":"Santay Island (Spanish Isla Santay) is a 2200 ha. island located in Guayas, Ecuador. It is part of the Durán Canton.","wpu":"Santay Island","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/AR_PN_COSTA03_SANTAY_AVITURISMO_White_Ibis_001_%2814180198845%29.jpg/960px-AR_PN_COSTA03_SANTAY_AVITURISMO_White_Ibis_001_%2814180198845%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Guayaquil Ecuador Temple","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-2.156246,"lo":-79.904877,"d":4661,"ds":"The Guayaquil Ecuador Temple is a temple of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints in Guayaquil, Ecuador. The groundbreaking ceremony was in August 1996, and the dedication in August 1999. In 2016, Caracol Radio reported that a Spanish real estate marketing company called Nuroa evaluated the Guayaquil temple to be worth US $14,456,000.","wpu":"Guayaquil Ecuador Temple","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/LDS_Temple_Ecuador.jpg/960px-LDS_Temple_Ecuador.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Guayaquil (Spanish pronunciation: [ɡwaʝaˈkil] ), officially Santiago de Guayaquil, is the capital of Guayas Province and the seat of Guayaquil Canton. It is the largest city in Ecuador and also the nation's economic capital and main port. The city is located on the west bank of the Guayas River, which flows into the Pacific Ocean at the Gulf of Guayaquil.","wpu":"Guayaquil","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Faro_de_Guayaquil.jpg/960px-Faro_de_Guayaquil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}